[Event "Scholars mate: the four move mate"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TQupggWr/BJcLQp6o"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today you learn scholars mate. Four moves, and the game is over. Open in the middle. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { That one little pawn opens two doors at once: one for your queen, one for your bishop. [%csl Gd1,Gf1] } (1. d4 { A fine opening, and a whole different world. But no queen road, no quick mate. } 1... d5 2. c4 { The queens gambit. Lovely chess, and nobody gets mated in four. }) (1. Nf3 { A good move. But look where the knight sat down: right in your own queens doorway. [%csl Rf3] } 1... d5 2. d4 { Solid chess, and the four move mate is off the menu. }) 1... e5 { He copies you. Now send the bishop to the square that stares at f7. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yf1c4] } 2. Bc4 { Straight down the diagonal at f7. That square has exactly one guard: his king. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (2. Qh5 { Queen first works too. But out in the open, everybody gets to poke at her. } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { The same picture, one move later. }) (2. Nf3 { The Italian is coming, and it is a fine opening. But the knight just blocked your queen. [%csl Rf3] } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 { Good chess. The mate road is shut, though. }) (2. Qf3 { She eyes f7 from here as well. But f6 is a door his knight can slam. } 2... Nf6 { Blocked already. The h5 square is the sneaky one. [%csl Gf6] }) 2... Nc6 { He guards his pawn and never sees it coming. Now fly the queen out to h5. [%cal Yd1h5] } 3. Qh5 { Two attackers on f7, one guard. And from h5 no knight can get in her way. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gh5f7,Gc4f7] } (3. Qf3 { She stares at f7 from here too. But he has a door to slam on f6. } 3... Nf6 { The knight steps into the doorway and the mate is gone. [%csl Gf6] }) (3. Nf3 { Sensible chess, and now you are simply playing the Italian. } 3... Bc5 { A good game for both. But the queen never got out. }) (3. Bxf7+ { The bishop cannot do this on his own. He simply takes it. } 3... Kxf7 { A whole bishop for one pawn, and his king strolls away. [%csl Rf7] }) (3. Nc3 { A normal, healthy developing move. It just has no interest in f7. } 3... Nf6 { Solid chess for both. The trap needed the queen on h5. }) (3. d3 { Quiet and sensible, and your queen is still sitting at home. } 3... Nf6 { A perfectly good game, and nothing like a four move mate. }) 3... Nf6 { And there it is. The most natural move on the board, and it loses on the spot — the pawn to g6 was the move that stopped this. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yh5f7,Gc4f7] } 4. Qxf7# { Mate! The queen lands on f7 and the bishop guards her. That is scholars mate. [%csl Gf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (4. Bxf7+ { Check! And it throws the mate away, because his knight is glaring at your queen. [%cal Rf6h5] } 4... Ke7 5. Qd1 Kxf7 { Your queen ran home and he ate the bishop. All that for nothing. }) (4. Qxe5+ { A check and a free pawn. But look who was guarding that square. [%cal Rc6e5] } 4... Nxe5 { Your queen for one pawn, and the mate that was there is gone. }) 1-0


[Event "Scholars mate: how black stops it"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TQupggWr/aVuD7jhe"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK1NR w KQkq - 2 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK1NR_w_KQkq_-_2_3"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Now you are black, and one day someone will try that on you. Do not panic. }
3. Qh5 { Out she comes. She and the bishop both want f7. Shoo her away with the g pawn. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg7g6] } 3... g6 { One small pawn, and it both blocks her road and pokes at her. She must move again. [%cal Gg6h5] } (3... Nf6 { The most natural move on the board, and it walks straight into mate. } 4. Qxf7# { The queen lands, the bishop guards her, and your king has nowhere to go. }) (3... d6 { It looks solid, and it does nothing at all about f7. } 4. Qxf7# { Same mate. Guarding the middle does not guard your front door. }) (3... Qe7 { Good defending. Your queen covers f7 and the mate is dead. } 4. d3 Nf6 { Perfectly fine. But your queen is standing in your own bishops way. }) (3... Qf6 { It guards f7 too. But your queen has taken the square your knight wanted. } 4. Nc3 Bc5 { Playable, and a little clumsy. The pawn move is the tidy one. }) (3... Nh6 { It covers f7, and the poor knight is stuck out on the rim. } 4. Nc3 g6 { You play the pawn move anyway, with a sadder knight. }) (3... d5 { It hits the bishop, and it costs a pawn and then a knight. } 4. Bxd5 Qf6 5. Bxc6+ bxc6 { He is a pawn up and your queen side is a wreck. Not worth it. }) 4. Qf3 { She slides across and stares at f7 all over again. Now block the road with a knight. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 { The door slams shut on f6. He moved his queen twice, you developed twice. You are better. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bg7 { A lovely square for the bishop, and the road to f7 is still wide open. } 5. Qxf7# { Mate. The knight had to block first. The bishop could have waited. }) (4... d6 { Solid looking, and it does not block the f road at all. } 5. Qxf7# { Mate again. She came down the f column, so block the f column. }) (4... Qe7 { This guards f7 and is perfectly good. } 5. Ne2 Nf6 { Fine chess. The knight does the same job and stays out of the way. }) (4... Qf6 { Offering a queen trade. It defends f7, and it steals your knights best square. } 5. Qxf6 Nxf6 { Queens off, danger over. Perfectly fine, and the pawn move was tidier. }) (4... Nh6 { It stops the mate, and the knight is miserable out on the rim. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Playable. The other knight was the one that wanted the job. }) (4... d5 { It attacks the bishop, and he simply takes it and keeps the threat. } 5. Bxd5 Qd7 { A pawn gone, and you are still busy defending f7. }) 0-1


[Event "Scholars mate: chase the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
